# The outer loop runs n_bootstrap=20 times. Each iteration randomly subsamples
# n_neurons=50 neurons from the population. This is critical when comparing
# across areas or depths that have different numbers of neurons - for example
# V1 has 5315 neurons and AL has 387. Without subsampling, V1 would always win
# just because it has more neurons. By always using 50 neurons we make fair
# comparisons. Running 20 iterations and averaging smooths out the randomness
# of which 50 neurons got picked.

# Inside each bootstrap we use 5-fold stratified cross validation. The 384 trials
# are split into 5 equal folds of ~77 trials each, with equal class proportions
# in each fold. In each fold the model trains on 4 folds (307 trials) and tests
# on the held out fold (77 trials). This repeats 5 times so every trial gets
# tested exactly once. We never test on data the model was trained on, which
# gives us an honest estimate of how well the decoder generalizes.

# Inside each fold we run a two-step pipeline:
# Step 1 - StandardScaler: normalizes each neuron's activity to have mean=0
# and std=1 across trials. This is important because different neurons have
# very different firing rates and without scaling high-firing neurons would
# dominate the decoder just because of their magnitude.
# Step 2 - LogisticRegression: learns weights for each of the 50 neurons.
# C=0.01 is strong regularization which penalizes large weights. This is
# important because we have more neurons (50) than we might expect given only
# 307 training trials, so regularization prevents overfitting.
# solver='saga' and multi_class='multinomial' means it solves the full 3-class
# problem jointly rather than doing one-vs-rest binary comparisons.

# Before the cross validation loop we create a shuffled version of the labels
# by randomly permuting y. We then run the exact same decoder on the shuffled
# labels. Because the labels no longer match the activity the decoder cannot
# learn anything real. The shuffle accuracy should land right at chance (0.333
# for 3 classes). If it does, we know our real accuracy is genuine and not
# an artifact of some statistical quirk. If shuffle is above chance something
# is wrong with our procedure.

# acc: mean accuracy across all bootstraps and folds (real)
# acc_std: standard deviation across bootstraps (how stable the estimate is)
# acc_shuf: mean accuracy with shuffled labels (should be ~0.333)
# acc_shuf_std: std of shuffle accuracy
# cm: mean normalized confusion matrix across bootstraps (real)
# cm_shuf: mean normalized confusion matrix for shuffle control
# classes: the class labels in the order used by the confusion matrix
